{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Third live reproduction — this now blocks real work, and the documented recovery does not surface the condition\n\nObserved while closing `dc28-2807-5b1d-4da7`. The close gate refused:\n\n```\nError: the completion close gate refused to certify dc28-2807-5b1d-4da7: the local\nticket store holds 1 committed ticket event(s) the shared store has never received\n(last delivery failure: push-transport-failed) (stale-store: push-pending).\n```\n\nThe store was not stale. Measured at that moment:\n\n```\nLOCAL : 358636592935607f5d08bccefaac4de3051844da\nREMOTE: 358636592935607f5d08bccefaac4de3051844da\nahead : 0\n```\n\nHEAD and `origin/tickets` at an identical SHA, zero commits ahead, while the gate asserted\none undelivered event.\n\n### The sentinel's exact persisted content\n\n```\n{'state': 'pending',\n 'reason': 'push-transport-failed',\n 'detail': 'fatal: unable to get password from user',\n 'remote_ref': 'origin/tickets',\n 'unpushed': '1',\n 'since': 1789713225.035315}\n```\n\nSo the originating failure was real — a rebar-internal push that could not authenticate. What\nis wrong is that **nothing ever falsifies it afterwards**. The events reached the remote by\nanother path, the clone is provably level, and the sentinel still decides. `_ref_divergence`\n(`freshness.py:65-92`) returns `None` for both \"level\" and \"ahead\", so a level store falls\nthrough to `elif pending` and the stale marker wins unchallenged.\n\n### Two findings beyond the original report\n\n**1. The marker is per-clone, but every clone acquires one, so switching clones is not a\nworkaround.** I retried the close from a second worktree's tracker, also measured 0 ahead, and\ngot the identical refusal. Each clone records its own failure because each one hit the same\nauthentication fault. The effect is indistinguishable from shared state: all clones wedge at\nonce.\n\nThe marker lives in the tracker's git dir, and because the tracker is itself a linked worktree\nthat resolves to the main repo's worktrees directory rather than to a `.git` subdirectory of\nthe tracker:\n\n```\n/Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.git/worktrees/-tickets-tracker/rebar-push-pending\n```\n\nWorth noting for anyone diagnosing this — the obvious place to look does not contain it.\n\n**2. The error's own recommended first step does not confirm the condition.** The message says:\n\n```\nRecovery:\n  rebar fsck                     # confirm the condition on THIS store\n```\n\n`rebar fsck` reported 97 issues on this store and **none of them was the push-pending\ncondition**. The step offered to confirm the diagnosis is silent about it, which sends an\noperator looking for a store-integrity problem that is not the cause. Either `fsck` should\nreport the sentinel, or the recovery text should not claim it confirms it.\n\n### Unwedge used\n\n`push_state.clear(tracker)` via the library API, guarded by an explicit 0-ahead check so it\ncannot mask a genuine backlog. The gate then proceeded. This is a workaround at the call site,\nnot a fix — the next authentication fault re-creates the marker and wedges the clone again.\n\n### Why this should be P1\n\nIt is no longer a latent diagnostic wart. It blocked a close whose change had already merged\nand whose completion gate had already returned PASS, and it did so on a healthy store. The\nmodule's own stated contract is that \"a broken diagnostic must never be able to block a\nhealthy gate\" — this is exactly that, reproduced three times, now with a measured blocking\nimpact and a recovery path that misdirects.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789715739795429512,"uuid":"2d58df01-07c9-4c31-9b4c-d19b7b209d3b"}